body:
## Construct

JSDoc types beyond `number|bigint|boolean|string|T[]|Array<T>|@typedef` are refused with `JSDoc type {…}: portable types are number, bigint, boolean, string, arrays T[] of them and @typedef data types`. That includes:

- tuples: `[string, number]`;
- record types: `{{path: string, bytes: number}}`;
- `object` and `Array<object>`;
- `Map<K, V>`, `Set<T>`, `Record<string, T>`;
- `T|null` (see the `null` issue);
- function types: `(x: string) => boolean`.

The standard optional-parameter syntax `@param {number} [count]` (and `[count=1]`) is refused as a **syntax** error: `@param needs a type and a name`. That happens even when the parameter has a default, which PR #196 already supports.

## Count in formal-ai

- `JSDoc type {…}`: **198** (the second largest class)
- `syntax: @param needs a type and a name`: **12**

The wider measurement counts 679 and 19.

## Minimal input

```js
/**
 * @param {string[]} names
 * @param {number} [count]
 * @returns {string}
 */
export function firstName(names, count = 1) {
  return count > 0 ? names[0] : '';
}

/**
 * @param {string[]} names
 * @param {number} count
 * @returns {[string, number]}
 */
export function firstWithCount(names, count) {
  return [names[0], count];
}
```

`firstName` is carried as a syntax error. `firstWithCount` is carried as `JSDoc type {[string, number]}`.

## Expected Rust

```rust
pub fn firstName(names: Vec<String>, count: f64) -> String {
    if count > 0f64 {
        crate::ml_array::at(&names, crate::ml_array::number_index(0f64))
    } else {
        String::from("")
    }
}

pub fn firstWithCount(names: Vec<String>, count: f64) -> (String, f64) {
    (crate::ml_array::at(&names, crate::ml_array::number_index(0f64)), count)
}
```

## Suggested direction

- `[name]` and `[name=default]` mark an optional or defaulted parameter. Accept them and use the default the source declares.
- `[A, B]` is a tuple, and a two-element array literal returned where a tuple is declared builds it.
- `{{a: A, b: B}}` is an anonymous record. Emit one struct per distinct shape (see the plain-object issue).
- `Map<K, V>` and `Set<T>` map to prelude types with JavaScript iteration order (see the `new` issue).

---

**Where this comes from.** [formal-ai](https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai) translates its JavaScript modules to Rust with `selfTranslate(source, 'JavaScript', 'Rust')` from meta-language `main` at `679a3b3c` (PR #196), through [`scripts/translate-js-rust.mjs`](https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/blob/a75bf377287664599d506921a30bd3bc37410022/scripts/translate-js-rust.mjs). In scope are 128 modules (`js/agentic/crate/*.mjs` and the meta reasoner): 249 of 2,369 top-level items translate and 2,120 are carried. The census counts the construct `translateProgram` names first for each carried item: [`data/meta/js-rust-translation.lino`](https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/blob/a75bf377287664599d506921a30bd3bc37410022/data/meta/js-rust-translation.lino). The per-item projections are in [`rust/tests/fixtures/js-rust-translation/`](https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/tree/a75bf377287664599d506921a30bd3bc37410022/rust/tests/fixtures/js-rust-translation). A wider measurement adds the agentic planner, the server and the browser worker, 232 more modules: 819 of 6,556 items translate there.

The repro above was checked with `selfTranslate` at `679a3b3c`. The umbrella for this work is #199, section 1. This issue is the one construct, with a minimal case to test against.
